Use
\[
\frac{p_1V_1}{T_1}=\frac{p_2V_2}{T_2}
\]
so
\[
p_2=p_1\frac{V_1}{V_2}\frac{T_2}{T_1}
=100\,\mathrm{kPa}\left(\frac{4.0}{2.5}\right)\left(\frac{360}{300}\right)
=192\,\mathrm{kPa}
\]

First find the amount:
\[
n=\frac{pV}{RT}
=\frac{(5.0\times10^5)(0.080)}{(8.31)(280)}
=17.2\,\mathrm{mol}
\]
Then
\[
m=nM=(17.2)(3.2\times10^{-2})=0.55\,\mathrm{kg}
\]

For a sealed rigid tank, \(n\) and \(V\) are constant, so
\[
\frac{p}{T}=\text{constant}
\]
Thus
\[
p_2=p_1\frac{T_2}{T_1}
=(2.4\times10^5)\frac{450}{300}
=3.6\times10^5\,\mathrm{Pa}
\]
The temperatures must be absolute temperatures in kelvin.
